I'm constructing a dataset of prompts on philosophical/conceptual topics. I'd like you to write me a prompt for this dataset. I'd like the prompts to be somewhat in the weeds of things, so that models can't just regurgitate something they've seen a million times in pretraining. At the same time, I'd like the prompt to be meaningful for a model that can't search online. In particular, the prompt shouldn't ask for bibliographies, reading lists, or citations -- a model without web access could only guess at those. But they also shouldn't refer super niche theories/ideas/facts that LLMs won't have properly memorized (especially small LLMs). To comply with these two competing objectives, you'll often have to inject some sort of new / niche ideas into the prompt.

{% if web_tools %}
You (in contrast to the model that the prompt is for) can use web search and web fetch, but most prompts should not need them: write from what you already know, and search or fetch only if the prompt genuinely requires real source material (e.g., quoting an actual passage) or would otherwise rest on a guessed-at specific.

{% if strict_quotes %}
One hard rule: never quote from memory. If your prompt puts anything in quotation marks that comes from a real text, you must web_fetch a source containing the passage and copy the quote from the fetched text -- search snippets aren't good enough. If you can't fetch a usable source, don't use a verbatim quote: paraphrase without quotation marks instead. Likewise, verify titles, dates, and attributions of specific works by search if the prompt leans on them; when in doubt, write the prompt so it doesn't depend on them.

{% endif %}
Quote a real text at length (more than a few paragraphs, up to the whole piece) only if it is in the public domain, it is under CC BY or CC0, or it was written by Caspar Oesterheld (who is building this dataset). From any other text, quote at most a few paragraphs.

Don't default to public-domain classics just because their status is easy to check: recent writing is often a better fit. You can quote at length from:
- EA Forum posts published on or after 1 December 2022 (all CC BY 4.0, so the date is enough; many research organizations cross-post there)
- gwern.net (CC0); Our World in Data and Distill articles (CC BY)
- arXiv papers whose abstract page lists CC BY 4.0 or CC0, and open-access journal articles whose page says "Creative Commons Attribution" (common in Synthese, Philosophical Studies, and Erkenntnis)
- US court opinions and other US federal government documents (public domain)
- Caspar Oesterheld's posts on casparoesterheld.com (not the guest posts by others)
These are not openly licensed, so quote at most a few paragraphs from them: LessWrong, the Alignment Forum, Substack newsletters (including Astral Codex Ten), Slate Star Codex, Cold Takes, 80,000 Hours, and the Stanford and Internet Encyclopedias of Philosophy. When you quote at length, say who wrote the text, its title, and where it appeared, as a real user would.

{% endif %}
